\title{PG1 - Can the arena binary get its warm-up in advance? Profile-guided optimisation (Oracle GraalVM, measurement only), and the same question for the JVM}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{platform}
\tag{arena}
\tag{performance}
\tag{benchmark}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/PG1.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Agent PG1, 2026-11-25, 00:00Z - 01:41Z. A measurement report. No module code was changed or published, no default was changed, \code{\verb~|scripts/~} was not touched, nothing was downloaded: the Oracle GraalVM 25.0.4 that was already unpacked under \code{\verb~|.toolchain/oracle-graalvm-25~} was used as it was. Everything of mine is under \code{\verb~|work/pg1/~} (builds, profiles, reports, logs, scripts); living summary \code{\verb~|work/status/prof-PG1.md~}. (Owner note 15 wants every note in the journal; this file is where its brief put it and moves with the others.)}

\p{The owner's question:}

\blockquote{
\p{"is it possible to release with warm up config jsons so graal warmup on start up and we bench directly the hot path in arena?"}
}

\p{\strong{Every table of this report is headed "Oracle GraalVM - measurement only, not a default build".} Oracle GraalVM is licensed under the GraalVM Free Terms and Conditions (GFTC), not an open-source licence; the default build, the arena build and every default script use GraalVM Community Edition only (owner notes 11 and 16). Rows named "CE" or "JVM launcher" are the Community Edition and stand beside the Oracle rows for comparison. All numbers are aarch64 (Cortex-X925); the arena's runner is x86-64, where nothing of this was measured.}

\subtree{
\title{0. The answer}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Yes, and the mechanism is profile-guided optimisation (PGO).} A binary that is compiled ahead of time has no warm-up phase to move; what a training run can give it is what a JIT learns while warming up (which branches are taken, which calls are hot), as \code{\verb~|.iprof~} files (JSON) that \code{\verb~|native-image --pgo=...~} compiles with. Only Oracle GraalVM has it (GraalVM CE 25: "Unrecognized option '--pgo-instrument'"); owner note 16 makes such a build an option, never the default.}
\li{\strong{The three numbers} - Mathlib, the machine with the candidate set \code{\verb~|default,eager,skipargs,congruence~}, 8 workers, one fresh process in the arena-shaped container; CE image as shipped -> Oracle GraalVM without a profile -> Oracle GraalVM with the Init profile (in brackets the two other training sets): \strong{instructions 1,687.6 G -> 1,705.6 G -> 1,483.2 G (1,528.2 G; 1,542.8 G), i.e. -12.1 \% (-9.4 \%; -8.6 \%)}; \strong{cold wall clock 17.82 s -> 16.73 s -> 15.57 s (15.81 s; 16.65 s)}; \strong{warm wall clock (passes 2-4 of one process) 17.11 s -> 17.49 s -> 15.18 s (16.20 s; 15.49 s)}.}
\li{\strong{On Mathlib a profile is worth 9 to 12 \% of the instructions and about a tenth of the wall clock; Oracle's compiler without one is worth nothing in instructions} (+-1 \%; 0 to 6 \% less CPU time). Over all eight corpora: instructions -11 to -15 \% with the Init profile and -9 to -12 \% with Std + cedar; CPU time -8 to -15 \% and cold wall clock -7 to -16 \% with Std + cedar, timed on all eight. The same with the machine's plain default set (Mathlib 2,806.0 G -> 2,511.7 G, -10.5 \%) and with the reference engine (Mathlib 8,560.8 G -> 7,503.7 G, -12.3 \%; -12 to -14 \% on the other seven, from a profile that was recorded on the machine).}
\li{\strong{"Bench directly the hot path": the image already does.} A cold Mathlib process takes 17.82 s, a later pass in the same process 17.11 s; in instructions the second pass of a two-pass process costs 1,697.9 G where a cold process costs 1,689.7 G. PGO makes the image's code better; it has no start-up phase to remove. The JVM is where the two differ: a warm Mathlib pass costs 1,180 to 1,240 G there, a cold process 1,369 to 1,425 G.}
\li{\strong{The cheapest training set is the best}: Init alone (21 s of training; what nano-lean, gruszka and pestka train on in their arena builds) gives 1,483.2 G (-12.1 \%) on Mathlib, Std + cedar 1,542.8 G (-8.6 \%), two Mathlib slices + Std 1,528.2 G (-9.4 \%), Mathlib itself 1,506.7 G (-10.7 \%) (not an admissible entry, and not better), init-prelude alone 1,587.0 G (-6.0 \%). A profile is 15 to 27 MB of JSON, \strong{2.6 to 4.8 MB gzip-compressed}.}
\li{\strong{Correctness}: on each of the arena's 270 tests the PGO image gives the status and the exit code of the CE image - with the shipped run line and with the candidate run line: 148 / 148 good accepted, 92 / 92 bad rejected, the two known \code{\verb~|either~} declines. Every image accepted all eight corpora in every configuration.}
\li{\strong{A stale profile degrades gently and silently}: a profile recorded 4.3 hours earlier (7 of 14 jars republished since) keeps a half to three quarters of the gain (Mathlib -4.1 \% against the CE image instead of -8.0 \% for a fresh profile of the same kind), a 17-hour-old profile of the reference engine three fifths (Init -8.9 \% instead of -15.3 \%); no stale or foreign profile made any measured cell slower than the CE image. The build output does not tell a stale profile from a fresh one.}
\li{\strong{The JVM cannot be handed its hot path with what JDK 25 has} (the AOT cache carries classes and method profiles, no compiled code; compiler-directive files and the Graal / Truffle options of the Community Edition steer compilation, they do not replace it: section 4). \strong{But "a JIT cannot win the arena's metric" is wrong at Mathlib size}: the JVM launcher as shipped (Community Edition, no profile, compiler threads counted) needs 1,368.7 G on eight quiet cores - less than the CE image and less than every PGO image, in the same wall clock (16.66 s) - and 1.5 to 3.8 times the image's instructions on everything short.}
\li{\strong{Shipping it} (section 5) takes an Oracle GraalVM download in the build that asks for it (GFTC), the Init profile in the repository (2.6 MB) or three minutes of training at build time as the leading arena entries do, a refresh of the profile with every release, and it buys \strong{no place on the board}: pestka needs 1,339.6 G on this host, the PGO image 1,483 G, the JVM launcher 1,355 to 1,425 G. Neither image is reproducible bit for bit (two builds from identical inputs have the same size and 81 to 86 \% different bytes, the CE image as well as the PGO image; the two PGO builds agree to 0.4 \% in instructions).}
\li{\strong{Not established}: anything on x86-64, the arena's architecture; whether a profile recorded on aarch64 serves an x86-64 build; timing differences below 5 \% (one run per cell). Section 6.}
}
}

