\tr{\th{image}\th{run line}\th{good: accepted / rejected / declined, error, timeout}\th{bad: rejected / accepted / other}\th{either: accepted / rejected / declined}\th{wrong verdicts}\th{instructions, all 270}\th{of them the 259 tests below 8 MiB}\th{status or exit code different from the CE image}}
\tr{\td{CE image (as shipped)}\td{as shipped (reference engine)}\td{148 / 0 / 0}\td{92 / 0 / 0}\td{9 / 19 / 2}\td{\strong{0}}\td{17,360.5 G}\td{1,288.59 G}\td{}}
\tr{\td{Oracle PGO A (Std + cedar)}\td{as shipped (reference engine)}\td{148 / 0 / 0}\td{92 / 0 / 0}\td{9 / 19 / 2}\td{\strong{0}}\td{15,271.8 G}\td{1,124.84 G}\td{none (270 of 270 equal)}}
\tr{\td{CE image (as shipped)}\td{\code{\verb~|-X engine=machine -X machine.switches=default,eager,skipargs,congruence~}}\td{148 / 0 / 0}\td{92 / 0 / 0}\td{11 / 17 / 2}\td{\strong{0}}\td{4,355.6 G}\td{623.54 G}\td{}}
\tr{\td{Oracle PGO A (Std + cedar)}\td{\code{\verb~|-X engine=machine -X machine.switches=default,eager,skipargs,congruence~}}\td{148 / 0 / 0}\td{92 / 0 / 0}\td{11 / 17 / 2}\td{\strong{0}}\td{3,923.9 G}\td{553.53 G}\td{none (270 of 270 equal)}}
\tr{\td{Oracle PGO B (Init)}\td{\code{\verb~|-X engine=machine -X machine.switches=default,eager,skipargs,congruence~}}\td{148 / 0 / 0}\td{92 / 0 / 0}\td{11 / 17 / 2}\td{\strong{0}}\td{3,812.9 G}\td{554.69 G}\td{none (270 of 270 equal)}}
\tr{\td{JVM launcher (CE, HotSpot C2)}\td{\code{\verb~|-X engine=machine -X machine.switches=default,eager,skipargs,congruence~}}\td{148 / 0 / 0}\td{92 / 0 / 0}\td{11 / 17 / 2}\td{\strong{0}}\td{5,019.9 G}\td{1,168.40 G}\td{none (270 of 270 equal)}}
}

\p{Every configuration of every image and launcher accepted the corpus it was given in every run of this report (201 measured runs of a corpus, none with a non-zero status). The two declined \code{\verb~|either~} tests are \code{\verb~|corner-cases/eta-struct-major-no-whnf~} and \code{\verb~|eta-struct-major-and-no-whnf~} (the official kernel does not terminate there; AR1.md 0.1). The \code{\verb~|either~} verdicts differ between the two run lines (9 / 19 against 11 / 17 accepted / rejected: the candidate set accepts two \code{\verb~|either~} tests that the reference engine rejects, the tolerated kind of owner note 13 (d)), not between the images. The suite containers ran beside quiet windows and were parked on two cores for most of their time: their instruction counts are valid, their times are not evidence. Even so no test used more than 22 \% of its arena time limit (\code{\verb~|perf/gf3-matrix-product~}, 15.3 s of 70 s, reference engine; with the candidate run line 7 \%; through the JVM launcher 17 \%: \code{\verb~|perf/ite-taken-branch-bad~}, 19.1 s of 110 s). "Below 8 MiB" are 259 of the 270 tests.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. Method}

\p{\strong{Jars.} The published jars as they were at 2026-11-25T00:02:50Z (\code{\verb~|work/pg1/logs/m2-jars-before-build.txt~}):}

\pre{\verb~|
rlvm-admit      398a117d4ad8   published 2026-11-24T18:01:13Z
rlvm-api        6ff40825ca1d   13:34:23Z
rlvm-cli        cac1433b287a   22:47:24Z   (Oracle variant: 10c4d6ccf815, compiled from the tree against Truffle 25.0.4;
                                            class files identical, only rlvm/cli/build.properties differs)
rlvm-driver     31fa08d9ce61   18:27:41Z
rlvm-export     dc8c4f1ef79a   23:47:05Z
rlvm-inductive  c15b14fffdd3   18:01:07Z
rlvm-level      75bb0db242c5   04:53:25Z
rlvm-load       b23a88e4c30b   23:47:10Z
rlvm-machine    b3adf60ca905   23:45:51Z
rlvm-main       21150203e39d   compiled from the tree by --published; byte-identical to the published jar
rlvm-nat        b1dda7562adb   06:53:51Z
rlvm-ref        a20c24a26b7c   22:58:43Z
rlvm-store      30979ee659b7   22:55:50Z
rlvm-truffle    ad3256d0751d   18:16:25Z
collections, jniutils, nativeimage, polyglot, truffle-api, truffle-compiler, truffle-runtime, word:
                25.5.5.0.3 (CE variant) / 25.0.4 (Oracle variant)
~}

\p{Default engine of the distribution: reference; the machine's provider priority is 10.}

\p{\strong{Build.} \code{\verb~|work/pg1/build.sh~} is \src{scripts/build.sh} as it was at 00:00Z with two lines changed: its own location, and a private build directory (\code{\verb~|work/pg1/build~} instead of \code{\verb~|work/build~}), so that a 9-minute PGO build did not hold the team's build lock. (\src{scripts/build.sh} itself was rewritten by its owner while this report was measured: the option is now \code{\verb~|--oracle-pgo~} and builds the one executable from profiles in \code{\verb~|profiles/~}. The command lines below are those of the copy.)}

\pre{\verb~|
bench/slot.sh -c ni -n 2 -- work/pg1/build.sh --published --pgo --opt 3 --dist work/pg1/dist \
    --train work/exports/std.ndjson --train work/exports/cedar.ndjson \
    --train-arg --threads --train-arg 8 --train-arg -X --train-arg engine=machine \
    --train-arg -X --train-arg machine.switches=default,eager,skipargs,congruence
~}

\p{It builds, from the same jars: \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm~} (CE 25.5.5.0.3, \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial~}, the machine's H10 inline lists: the image as shipped), the instrumented image (Oracle 25.0.4, \code{\verb~|--pgo-instrument~}), the profile A and \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-pgo~} (\code{\verb~|--pgo=~}). The other Oracle images were built by \code{\verb~|work/pg1/scripts/oracle-image.sh~} from the same Oracle-variant jars with exactly the arguments \code{\verb~|build.sh~} gives its Oracle images (\code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial --no-fallback -R:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError -R:MaxHeapSize=13g -H:+ReportExceptionStackTraces~}) plus \code{\verb~|--pgo=<files>~}, or nothing (the control), or \code{\verb~|-H:-MLProfileInference~} (the second control: Oracle GraalVM infers branch probabilities with a built-in model when it has no profile, "PGO: ML-inferred" in its build output). \strong{The Oracle images have no H10 inline lists} (\code{\verb~|build.sh~} gives them to the CE image only); the control therefore differs from the CE image in the compiler, its version and the lists.}

\p{\strong{Training} (\code{\verb~|build.sh~}, section "PGO", step c; \code{\verb~|work/pg1/scripts/train.sh~} for the further variants): the instrumented image runs \code{\verb~|--truffle-probe~} and then checks each training export with \code{\verb~|RLVM_EXIT=exit~}, \code{\verb~|--stats~}, \code{\verb~|--threads 8 -X engine=machine -X machine.switches=default,eager,skipargs,congruence~} (the load is pipelined by the checker's own rule); each process writes one \code{\verb~|.iprof~}. A = Std + cedar, B = Init, C = two Mathlib module cones (\code{\verb~|work/exports/slices/good/Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.CauchyIntegral.cone.ndjson~}, \code{\verb~|...FieldTheory.Galois.Basic.cone...~}) + Std, P = init-prelude, M = Mathlib itself. C contains declarations of the benchmark (their cones are part of Mathlib) and M is the benchmark: both are here to see what training data can do, not as candidates.}

\p{\strong{Measurement.} One run = one fresh arena-shaped container (\code{\verb~|bench/arena-docker.sh --arena-limits --no-network~}: the 8 performance cores, 16 GB) and in it \src{bench/measure.sh} = the arena's \code{\verb~|perf stat -e instructions:u ... time ... sh -c RUN~} with \code{\verb~|$IN~}; RUN is the \code{\verb~|run:~} line of \src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml} as it was at 00:05Z (\code{\verb~|--threads 8~}, the status mapping), executed verbatim with a working directory whose \code{\verb~|work/dist/bin/rlvm~} is the image under test, with the engine options inserted after \code{\verb~|--threads 8~}:}

\ul{
\li{"candidate set": \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine -X machine.switches=default,eager,skipargs,congruence~}}
\li{"plain default set": \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~}}
\li{"reference engine": \code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~} (in the suites: the line as shipped, which runs the reference engine)}
}

\p{Instruction counts need no quiet window; every CPU-seconds and wall-clock figure was taken inside a \src{bench/quiet.py} window (\code{\verb~|work/pg1/scripts/window.sh~}: one measurement at a time on the host, the rest of the session off the measurement cores; one Mathlib process per window, no window above five minutes): 27 windows between 00:11Z and 01:37Z, in turn with another agent's. Warm passes: \code{\verb~|bench/warm.sh --launcher native --passes 4 --load default~} in windows of their own (\code{\verb~|work/pg1/scripts/warm.sh~}; the process runs on the host, pinned to the same eight cores). Runs outside a window ("count runs", and the suites) give instructions and peak RSS only; they shared the eight cores with each other and were moved onto two spare cores during every window of anybody. The "JVM launcher" rows are \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm~} of the same distribution behind a two-line wrapper named \code{\verb~|rlvm~} (with \code{\verb~|-XX:-EnableJVMCI -XX:AOTCache=FILE~} for the AOT rows, \code{\verb~|-XX:-TieredCompilation~} for the untiered row), so that the same run line runs them. A cell that was measured more than once shows the count of the quiet run; the largest difference between two counts of one cell of a native image is 0.3 \% with the machine (17 cells with two or more runs) and 1.2 \% with the reference engine (9 cells), whose work depends more on how its workers interleave.}

\p{\strong{Host.} aarch64, 10 Cortex-X925 + 10 Cortex-A725, 121 GB, shared with about ten other agents for the whole session (load average 25 to 60). The heap cap of every image is its built-in 13 GB (\code{\verb~|-R:MaxHeapSize=13g~}); no \code{\verb~|-Xmx~} was given, as in the arena.}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. Training data, foreign profiles, stale profiles}

\subtree{
\title{3.1 The choice of training data}

\p{\strong{Oracle GraalVM - measurement only, not a default build.} What the choice of training data does: instructions of a cold process with the candidate set. Every profile = \code{\verb~|--truffle-probe~} + the exports named, trained with the machine (candidate set, 8 workers) unless the row says otherwise.}

\table{
\tr{\th{image}\th{trained on}\th{profile files}\th{raw bytes}\th{gzip -9 bytes}\th{Mathlib}\th{vs CE}\th{cedar}\th{vs CE}\th{Init}\th{vs CE}\th{navier-stokes-euler}\th{vs CE}}
\tr{\td{CE image (as shipped)}\td{-}\td{-}\td{-}\td{-}\td{1,687.6 G}\td{}\td{204.1 G}\td{}\td{81.2 G}\td{}\td{527.2 G}\td{}}
\tr{\td{Oracle, no profile}\td{- (ML-inferred branch probabilities, Oracle's default)}\td{-}\td{-}\td{-}\td{1,705.6 G}\td{+1.1 \%}\td{204.9 G}\td{+0.4 \%}\td{82.0 G}\td{+1.0 \%}\td{520.3 G}\td{-1.3 \%}}
\tr{\td{Oracle, no profile, ML inference off}\td{- (\code{\verb~|-H:-MLProfileInference~})}\td{-}\td{-}\td{-}\td{1,741.0 G}\td{+3.2 \%}\td{209.0 G}\td{+2.4 \%}\td{83.3 G}\td{+2.6 \%}\td{-}\td{}}
\tr{\td{Oracle PGO P (init-prelude)}\td{init-prelude (3.8 MB)}\td{2}\td{14,440,954}\td{2,538,436}\td{1,587.0 G}\td{-6.0 \%}\td{192.3 G}\td{-5.8 \%}\td{75.6 G}\td{-6.9 \%}\td{518.5 G}\td{-1.7 \%}}
\tr{\td{Oracle PGO B (Init)}\td{Init (348 MB)}\td{2}\td{14,781,540}\td{2,599,655}\td{1,483.2 G}\td{-12.1 \%}\td{174.7 G}\td{-14.4 \%}\td{70.3 G}\td{-13.4 \%}\td{450.7 G}\td{-14.5 \%}}
\tr{\td{Oracle PGO A (Std + cedar)}\td{Std + cedar (584 + 905 MB)}\td{3}\td{21,008,555}\td{3,754,719}\td{1,542.8 G}\td{-8.6 \%}\td{181.7 G}\td{-11.0 \%}\td{73.3 G}\td{-9.7 \%}\td{463.3 G}\td{-12.1 \%}}
\tr{\td{Oracle PGO A, second build}\td{the same profile files, built a second time}\td{3}\td{21,008,555}\td{3,754,719}\td{1,548.9 G}\td{-8.2 \%}\td{181.5 G}\td{-11.1 \%}\td{73.2 G}\td{-9.9 \%}\td{-}\td{}}
\tr{\td{Oracle PGO C (2 Mathlib slices + Std)}\td{two Mathlib module cones (313 + 174 MB) + Std}\td{4}\td{26,884,718}\td{4,835,047}\td{1,528.2 G}\td{-9.4 \%}\td{180.1 G}\td{-11.7 \%}\td{72.5 G}\td{-10.7 \%}\td{459.8 G}\td{-12.8 \%}}
\tr{\td{Oracle PGO M (Mathlib itself)}\td{Mathlib itself (the benchmark: not an admissible entry)}\td{2}\td{14,902,646}\td{2,633,734}\td{1,506.7 G}\td{-10.7 \%}\td{178.9 G}\td{-12.3 \%}\td{72.0 G}\td{-11.3 \%}\td{454.6 G}\td{-13.8 \%}}
}

\p{Reading.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Any real export gives most of it, and the smallest was the best.} The three admissible sets end 8.6 to 12.1 \% below the CE image on Mathlib and 4 \% apart from each other (1,483 G with Init, 1,528 G and 1,543 G with the larger sets). That order is not build noise: a second image built from the same profile files as A differs from the first by 0.4 \% on Mathlib (1,548.9 against 1,542.8 G) and by 0.1 to 0.3 \% on cedar and Init. With Init the image is 11 to 15 \% below the CE image on all eight corpora, with Std + cedar 9 to 12 \% (table 1.2). Why Init does better than the larger sets was not investigated; it is an observation on five profiles, not a rule.}
\li{\strong{Training on the benchmark itself is not better.} The profile recorded on Mathlib gives 1,506.7 G, -10.7 \% against the CE image on Mathlib: between the Init profile and the larger sets, and behind Init on cedar, Init and navier-stokes-euler as well. So the choice of training data has no ceiling above the Init profile to offer, and nothing is given away by not training on the ranking corpus.}
\li{\strong{init-prelude alone} (1,840 declarations, 3.8 MB - a training input small enough to keep in the repository, one second of training): 1,587.0 G on Mathlib (-6.0 \%), 192.3 G on cedar (-5.8 \%), 75.6 G on Init (-6.9 \%).}
\li{\strong{Without a profile, Oracle's compiler gives the machine nothing in instructions} (+-1 \% on the eight corpora with its built-in branch-probability model; +3.2 \% on Mathlib, +2.4 \% on cedar, +2.6 \% on Init with the model switched off) and 0 to 6 \% less CPU time; for the reference engine it is worth 3 to 5 \% of the instructions. The control is not a pure compiler comparison: the CE image has the machine's inline lists, the Oracle images do not (section 2).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.2 A profile of another configuration or another engine, and a profile of older code}

\p{\strong{Oracle GraalVM - measurement only, not a default build.} Foreign and stale profiles: instructions of a cold process. "vs fresh" compares with the image whose profile was trained on the same export with today's jars and the configuration of the row.}

\table{
\tr{\th{configuration}\th{image}\th{Mathlib}\th{vs CE}\th{vs fresh}\th{cedar}\th{vs CE}\th{vs fresh}\th{Init}\th{vs CE}\th{vs fresh}}
\tr{\td{machine, candidate set}\td{CE image (as shipped)}\td{1,687.6 G}\td{}\td{}\td{204.1 G}\td{}\td{}\td{81.2 G}\td{}\td{}}
\tr{\td{machine, candidate set}\td{Oracle, no profile}\td{1,705.6 G}\td{+1.1 \%}\td{}\td{204.9 G}\td{+0.4 \%}\td{}\td{82.0 G}\td{+1.0 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{machine, candidate set}\td{Oracle PGO B (Init)}\td{1,483.2 G}\td{-12.1 \%}\td{}\td{174.7 G}\td{-14.4 \%}\td{}\td{70.3 G}\td{-13.4 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{machine, candidate set}\td{Oracle PGO BR (Init: the machine's run + a reference-engine run)}\td{1,526.0 G}\td{-9.6 \%}\td{+2.9 \%}\td{179.7 G}\td{-11.9 \%}\td{+2.9 \%}\td{72.2 G}\td{-11.0 \%}\td{+2.7 \%}}
