\tr{\td{reference engine}\td{Oracle PGO R (init-prelude + Init, trained with the reference engine)}\td{-}\td{}\td{}\td{448.5 G}\td{-15.9 \%}\td{}\td{213.2 G}\td{-15.3 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{reference engine}\td{Oracle PGO BR (Init: the machine's run + a reference-engine run)}\td{-}\td{}\td{}\td{447.8 G}\td{-16.0 \%}\td{-0.2 \%}\td{212.7 G}\td{-15.5 \%}\td{-0.2 \%}}
\tr{\td{reference engine}\td{Oracle PGO A (Std + cedar)}\td{7,503.7 G}\td{-12.3 \%}\td{}\td{466.1 G}\td{-12.6 \%}\td{+3.9 \%}\td{219.5 G}\td{-12.8 \%}\td{+2.9 \%}}
\tr{\td{reference engine}\td{Oracle PGO S1 (STALE: the profile of 07:5xZ, reference engine)}\td{-}\td{}\td{}\td{487.8 G}\td{-8.5 \%}\td{+8.8 \%}\td{229.3 G}\td{-8.9 \%}\td{+7.5 \%}}
}

\p{Reading.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Train with the configuration that ships; it covers the smaller one too.} The Init profile recorded with the machine's plain default set, used with the candidate set, gives -8.0 \% on Mathlib where the candidate set's own profile gives -12.1 \% (cedar -11.0 against -14.4 \%, Init -10.1 against -13.4 \%): a third of the gain is lost. The other way round nothing is: with the plain default set the candidate set's profile is 1 to 2 \% \em{better} than the default set's own (Mathlib -10.5 against -9.3 \%) - the candidate set runs the default set's code and more.}
\li{\strong{A profile of the other engine still helps.} Profile A was recorded with the machine; with the reference engine its image needs 7,503.7 G on Mathlib where the CE image needs 8,560.8 G (-12.3 \%; -12 to -14 \% on the other seven corpora; a profile recorded with the reference engine itself on init-prelude + Init: -15 to -16 \% on Init, Std and cedar): the two engines share the loader, the store, the admission of inductive types and the arithmetic, and no engine got slower from a profile that was not its own. The mis-training hazard of [study-graalvm-platform](study-graalvm-platform) 7.4 (an image slower than the untrained one on a path the training never took) did not show in any cell of this report.}
\li{\strong{A profile with a run of each engine.} The two files of B plus the reference engine's run on Init (row BR; the shape of the profile that is in \code{\verb~|profiles/~} now): the reference engine gets all that its own profile gives (Init -15.5 \%, cedar -16.0 \%), and the machine gives up a fifth of its gain (Mathlib -9.6 \% against the CE image instead of -12.1 \%, cedar -11.9 \% instead of -14.4 \%, Init -11.0 \% instead of -13.4 \%). The machine's run alone still gives the reference engine 12 to 13 \%. So for a binary whose checking engine is the machine the machine's run alone is the better profile (by 2.9 \% of Mathlib's instructions); both runs are the better profile while the reference engine is the default engine.}
\li{\strong{A stale profile: the machine.} The instrumented image was built from the jars of the distribution of 19:41Z (machine \code{\verb~|78c74ca92785~}; 7 of the 14 RLVM jars were republished before 00:02Z, the machine's at least six times), trained on Init with that machine and its default set, and the profile was given to today's jars. Against a fresh profile recorded the same way (Init, plain default set) it keeps a half to three quarters of the gain: with the plain default set Mathlib -6.7 \% against the CE image instead of -9.3 \%, cedar -5.6 \% instead of -12.1 \%, Init -7.2 \% instead of -11.2 \%; with the candidate set Mathlib -4.1 \% instead of -8.0 \%, cedar -4.3 \% instead of -11.0 \%, Init -5.9 \% instead of -10.1 \%. No cell became slower than the CE image.}
\li{\strong{A stale profile: the reference engine.} The profile of the PGO build of 07:5xZ ([design-platform-document](design-platform-document), Part II: init-prelude + Init with the reference engine of 06:54Z; since then the RLVM code in a profile grew from 1,468 to 3,329 methods) on today's jars, against a profile recorded today the same way: Init -8.9 \% against the CE image instead of -15.3 \%, Std -9.3 \% instead of -15.6 \%, cedar -8.5 \% instead of -15.9 \%. Seventeen hours and a doubled code base left three fifths of the gain.}
\li{\strong{Nothing warns.} The build output of the stale profiles reads like that of a fresh one ("profile relevance" 98.7 \% and 97.9 \% against 99.0 \% and 98.8 \%; "applicability" 27.1 \% and 23.2 \% against 28.1 \% and 25.6 \%: table 1.5), and by method identity and bytecode index 87 \% of the branch contexts of a fresh profile are also in the profile that is 4.3 hours old (94 \% by executed count) and 71 \% in the one that is 17 hours old, while two fresh profiles of the same jars recorded with different switch sets share 95 \% (\code{\verb~|work/pg1/scripts/iprof-overlap.py~}). The counters of an edited method stay attached to its old bytecode indices; the compiler cannot know.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.3 What a profile is tied to}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The compiler's profile format.} \code{\verb~|native-image~} has \code{\verb~|-H:±PGOIgnoreVersionCheck~} ("Prevent PGO run from failing if the profile version is not correct, and apply profiles on a best-effort basis"): so, by the option's own description, a profile whose format version (\code{\verb~|"version": "1.0.0"~} in the file) is not the compiler's fails the build by default. Not tested: the profiles here were written and read by the same Oracle GraalVM 25.0.4.}
\li{\strong{The names and the bytecode of the code it describes.} A profile is a list of types and methods by name and signature and of counters keyed by a calling context: \code{\verb~|method:bytecode index < caller:bytecode index < ...~}. A method that was renamed or removed loses its entries (they are dropped: "relevance" in the build output of \code{\verb~|-H:+PGOPrintProfileQuality~}); a method whose body was edited keeps them, attached to whatever now sits at the old bytecode indices. So the build output of a stale profile looks like that of a fresh one (table 1.5: relevance 98.7 \% against 99.0 \%), and the only test is a measurement.}
\li{\strong{The JDK and Truffle classes of the GraalVM version} (four in ten of a profile's branch contexts and more than nine in ten of the methods it names are not RLVM code).}
\li{\strong{Not the architecture, by its format} (names and bytecode indices; nothing of the machine code). Whether an x86-64 build takes an aarch64 profile with the same effect could not be tested here.}
\li{The instrumented image is compiled at \code{\verb~|-O2~} whatever level is asked for (its build output says so). Whether a profile depends on other flags of the final build (the inline lists, \code{\verb~|-march~}) was not tested.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. The same question for the JVM}

\subtree{
\title{4.1 What exists (verified on the JDK of \code{\verb~|.toolchain/graalvm-ce-25~}, 25.0.5; the wall-clock measurements are J1's)}

\ul{
\li{\strong{JDK 25's AOT cache} (JEP 483 / 514 / 515: \code{\verb~|-XX:AOTCacheOutput=FILE~} in a training run, \code{\verb~|-XX:AOTCache=FILE~} afterwards) is the JVM's "warm-up configuration": the classes of the training run, loaded and linked, and its method profiles, so that the JIT compiles from a ripe profile at once. \strong{It carries no compiled code} (J1.md 3.1: the cache's code region is 0 bytes; this JDK has only the diagnostic switches \code{\verb~|AOTAdapterCaching~} and \code{\verb~|AOTStubCaching~}, both off, for adapters and stubs). It works only with \code{\verb~|-XX:-EnableJVMCI~}, is tied to the JDK build and to the jars (size, modification time, class path order: J1.md 3.4) and is 20.4 MB here (Std x 4 passes: 106 s on the loaded host; Init x 4: 71 s). J1 measured it by wall clock: 0.4 to 1.1 s off the cold wall, every later pass 5 to 9 \% slower in 11 of 11 comparisons; by the owner's metric it does not belong in the launcher.}
\li{\strong{Compiler-directive files} (\code{\verb~|-XX:CompilerDirectivesFile=FILE~}, JSON; a \em{diagnostic} option in this JDK, it needs \code{\verb~|-XX:+UnlockDiagnosticVMOptions~}) and \code{\verb~|-XX:CompileCommandFile~} give C1 and C2 instructions per method: exclude it, force or forbid an inlining, log, switch an intrinsic, compile in the foreground. They say how a method is compiled, not that it need not be: no profile and no code is in such a file, and the compiler threads do the same work. (J1.md 2.2: no inlining flag moves the warm pass of the machine either.)}
\li{\strong{Graal as the JIT} (\code{\verb~|-XX:+UseJVMCICompiler~}, Community Edition): none of its 74 options (\code{\verb~|-XX:+JVMCIPrintProperties~}) saves or loads profiles or code. (The image builder's \code{\verb~|-H:SaveProfiles~} / \code{\verb~|-H:LoadProfiles~}, in CE as well, replay the profile of ONE compilation for debugging; that is not PGO.)}
\li{\strong{Truffle's engine cache} (\code{\verb~|engine.CacheStore~} / \code{\verb~|engine.CacheLoad~}: a file with the engine's code, so that a later process starts with it): checked, not assumed. The shipped image refuses it ("Native image does not support auxilliary engine caching. Enable with -H:+AuxiliaryEngineCache at image build time"). \strong{GraalVM CE 25.5.5.0.3 has that build option}, and a CE image built with \code{\verb~|-H:+AuxiliaryEngineCache -H:ReservedAuxiliaryImageBytes=1073741824~} (both experimental) stored the engine of \code{\verb~|rlvm --truffle-probe~} (81 kB) and loaded it in a second process ("Loaded image ... Engine from image successfully patched with new options") - so it is not an Oracle-only feature in this version, as far as storing and loading go. Whether compiled code comes back was not shown: the probe builds its root node anew and compiled it again. It is moot for the release: the cache holds Truffle-compiled code, and the machine's Truffle tier (switch \code{\verb~|t2~}) is off because it does not pay in steady state ([rep-truffle-T3](rep-truffle-T3), section 1), not because of its warm-up.}
}

\p{So no: nothing in the Community Edition lets a JVM process start with its hot path compiled. The compilation happens in every process, and the arena counts the compiler threads' instructions.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.2 The number that was missing: a cold JVM process in the arena's metric}

\p{Nobody had counted the instructions of the JVM launcher with the machine on Mathlib (PLATFORM.md, Part I, has the counts below Mathlib only). They are here, with the launcher as shipped, without its C1 tiers, and with an AOT cache trained with the candidate set (\code{\verb~|work/pg1/scripts/aot-train.sh~}):}

