\tr{\td{reachability}\td{machine, candidate set}\td{Oracle PGO B (Init)}\td{14.5 G}\td{-10.9 \%}\td{-}\td{-}\td{631}\td{1 (0)}\td{14.5}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{machine, candidate set}\td{JVM launcher (CE, HotSpot C2)}\td{59.2 G}\td{+263.0 \%}\td{-}\td{-}\td{1,371}\td{2 (0)}\td{59.2 / 62.4}}
}

\p{Reading.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{On everything short the JIT is the bill.} With the candidate set a cold JVM process needs 185 to 197 G on Init where the image needs 81 G, 307 to 340 G against 204 G on cedar, 59 to 62 G against 16 G on reachability; the two meet at the size of navier-stokes-euler and cslib (532 to 546 G against 527 G; 579 against 569 G).}
\li{\strong{On Mathlib the bill is paid back inside the run.} 1,368.7 G on eight quiet cores, against 1,687.6 G for the CE image and 1,483.2 G for the best admissible PGO image - with the same CPU time (125.1 against 127.5 s) and a second less wall clock (16.66 against 17.82 s), and with the plain default set as well (2,197 against 2,806 G). C2 compiles the machine better than the ahead-of-time compiler does even when that has a profile: one warm Mathlib pass costs 1,180 to 1,240 G on the JVM and 1,698 G in the image (table 1.4). The price is in the last column: \strong{the JVM's count depends on how its compiler threads are scheduled} - 1,411 and 1,425 G in two runs that were squeezed onto two cores beside other containers, 4 \% above the quiet run, where an image repeats to 0.1 \%.}
\li{\strong{The AOT cache halves the JVM's handicap on short runs and does nothing on Mathlib}: Init 196.8 -> 121.5 G, cedar 340.0 -> 251.2 G (it removes the phase in which C1 code collects the profile, not the C2 compilations); Mathlib 1,364.8 against 1,368.7 G in quiet windows, 1,377 and 1,397 against 1,411 and 1,425 G in count runs. Without the C1 tiers (\code{\verb~|-XX:-TieredCompilation~}: interpreter and C2 only) a cold Mathlib process needs 1,458.3 G (a count run); with the Init cache as well 1,355.5 G in a quiet window (1,357.2 G in a count run) - the lowest count of this report - at the price of the wall clock: 21.16 s.}
\li{\strong{So "a JIT cannot win the arena's metric" is true of the short tests and false of the one that ranks} (and of con-leche: 381 to 408 G against 477 G). All 270 tests through the JVM launcher, under the arena's time limits: the same status and exit code as the CE image on every test, no timeout (the closest: 19 s of 110 s in a container squeezed onto two cores), 0.22 s per small test where the image needs 0.014 s, 1.87 times the image's instructions over the tests below 8 MiB (table 1.6).}
\li{\strong{What it does not give}: a place (1,355 to 1,369 G is 1 to 2 \% above pestka's 1,339.6 G on this host: sixth, like the image), one executable (owner note 16: the JVM launcher is a script, 22 jars and the JDK the build downloads), a count that repeats, or the image's memory (5,400 to 5,800 MiB against 4,100). And it is no more "the hot path directly" than before: a cold JVM process still spends a tenth to a sixth of its instructions getting warm (table 1.4), and JDK 25 has nothing that hands a fresh process the compiled code.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. What shipping it would take (for the owner to decide)}

\p{Owner note 16 (2026-11-24, recorded while this report was measured) already sets the frame: the Oracle GraalVM build with a shipped profile is an OPTION of the build, never its default, and the maintainer accepts the licence on the development machine for experiments. The list below is what the measurements add to that.}

\ol{
\li{\strong{What would be in the repository: a profile, about 2.6 MB.} For the Init profile (variant B) two files: \code{\verb~|probe.iprof~} (8.65 MB of JSON, 1.47 MB gzip-compressed: the profile of \code{\verb~|--truffle-probe~}, i.e. of the Truffle runtime compiler inside the image) and \code{\verb~|train-1.iprof~} (6.13 MB, 1.13 MB compressed: the checker on Init); together 14.8 MB raw, 2.60 MB compressed. \code{\verb~|native-image --pgo=~} reads gzip-compressed profiles (its help text says so, and an image built here from the two compressed files reports "PGO: user-provided" and has the size of the image built from the plain files, 46,663,800 bytes; it needs 70.4 G on Init and 174.6 G on cedar, the plain-file image 70.3 G and 174.7 G). Std + cedar: 3.75 MB compressed; three training exports: 4.84 MB. A profile is not a configuration a user can read or edit, and every refresh adds its 2.6 MB to the history of the repository.}
\li{\strong{Which profile: Init, recorded with the configuration that ships.} It is the best of the admissible ones on Mathlib and on each of the other seven corpora in this measurement, its training takes 21 s, it does not contain the ranking corpus, and it is what the three arena entries that use PGO train on. A profile recorded with another switch set than the one that ships loses a third of the gain (section 3.2). (While this report was measured, the owner of the packaging added the optional build \code{\verb~|scripts/build.sh --oracle-pgo~} and a first \code{\verb~|profiles/~} directory, id \code{\verb~|88f067bfcb35~}: \code{\verb~|--truffle-probe~}, Init with the default configuration and Init with the candidate set, 3.6 MB compressed, with a provenance file. That build was not measured here; row BR of table 3.2 has the same shape of training.)}
\li{\strong{A refresh with every release, and a guard.} A profile is tied to the bytecode of the jars and to the Oracle GraalVM version (section 3.3). Measured (section 3.2): a profile that was 4.3 hours old kept a half to three quarters of its gain, one that was 17 hours old three fifths, and neither made anything slower than the CE image - at this project's pace of change, which a released code base will not have. Nothing in the build output tells a fresh profile from a stale one, so the profile needs its own provenance file (the jar hashes it was recorded with, the GraalVM version, the training command) and the optional build should say when the jars it compiles are not the jars of the profile. Regenerating costs one instrumented image (164 s here) and the training (21 s).}
\li{\strong{The Oracle GraalVM download, in the build that asks for it.} The archives are 380.5 MB (linux-x64) and 351.6 MB (linux-aarch64), downloadable without a login from version-pinned URLs with published checksums ([study-graalvm-platform](study-graalvm-platform), section 7.3; not fetched again for this report). The build also needs the Truffle jars of that GraalVM's version (25.0.4 instead of 25.5.5.0.3): eight more third-party jars to pin by checksum, and \code{\verb~|rlvm-cli~} compiled against them (its class files come out identical).}
\li{\strong{The licence, in one sentence} (no legal advice): the GraalVM Free Terms and Conditions permit, free of charge, internal use of the unmodified program "for the purposes of developing, testing, prototyping and demonstrating your applications, and running the Program for Your own personal use or internal business operations" and its redistribution without a fee, and deem the parts of it that a Native Image output contains an unmodified program under the same terms - it is not an open-source licence. Text: \<html:a>[href]{\verb~|https://www.oracle.com/downloads/licenses/graal-free-license.html~}{https://www.oracle.com/downloads/licenses/graal-free-license.html} (the same text is \code{\verb~|LICENSE.txt~} in the archive). Owner note 16 accepts it on the development machine for experiments. \strong{In the arena the build would be run, and the licence accepted, by the arena's operators.} That is a question to put to them before a definition with such a build line is submitted; nothing in this repository can answer it.}
\li{\strong{What the arena's rules say.} The arena's README (\code{\verb~|refs/lean-kernel-arena/README.md~}) has no rule about downloads in the build step or about prebuilt artefacts; it says "Checkers are not run in a sandbox. We assume good faith from all contributors", and the build time is not measured ([study-arena-and-export-format](study-arena-and-export-format), "Build"). Precedent, from the definitions in \code{\verb~|refs/lean-kernel-arena/checkers/~}: \strong{the entries ranked 1, 2 and 5 are PGO builds that train at build time on Init} - nano-lean (\code{\verb~|scripts/pgo.sh~}: a sha256-pinned \code{\verb~|init.ndjson.gz~} from a release asset, \code{\verb~|-Cprofile-generate~}, one run over Init, \code{\verb~|-Cprofile-use~}), gruszka and pestka (a sha256-pinned \code{\verb~|init.susz~} from a dataset host, \code{\verb~|scripts/build-pgo.sh~}, \code{\verb~|-C target-cpu=native~}); kefir-wasm installs a toolchain with curl; RLVM's own definition downloads GraalVM CE and Maven. None of them ships a recorded profile, none ships a prebuilt binary, and all of their compilers are open source.}
\li{\strong{So there are two ways to build it, and the arena's precedent is the other one.} (a) Owner note 16: the profile is in the repository, the optional build needs no training, costs no more time than the CE build (table 1.5) and can be stale. (b) As the leaders do: the build makes the instrumented image, trains on a pinned Init export that it downloads (348 MB; the repository has none) and builds again - about 3 minutes more on this host, never stale, nothing recorded in the repository but a checksum. The measurements of this report are the same for both.}
\li{\strong{The image is not reproducible bit for bit.} Two builds from the same jars, the same profile files and the same command line have the same size and different bytes - the PGO image (46,991,480 bytes, 81 \% of them different) and the CE image (68,159,608 bytes, 86 \% different) alike, so it is the image builder, not the profile. The two PGO builds agree to 0.4 \% in instructions on Mathlib and 0.1 to 0.3 \% on cedar and Init. A release can publish the hash of the binary it built; nobody can confirm that hash by building again.}
\li{\strong{What it buys on the arena's board: 12 \%, and no place.} In this host's counts the candidate set stands at 1,687.6 G with the CE image; the next entry ahead is pestka, which needs 1,339.6 G on this host (three runs, \code{\verb~|bench/baselines.json~}; 1,475.3 G published on x86-64), the next behind is kefir (2,577.3 G published). With the Init profile the image needs 1,483.2 G: 144 G (10 \%) short of pestka, the same sixth place by AR1's projection (AR1.md 0.3; fifth only at the favourable end of its conversion range). The JVM launcher stands at 1,355 to 1,369 G on eight quiet cores (as shipped; with an AOT cache; with the cache and without its C1 tiers): 1 to 2 \% short of pestka, with a count that moves by 4 \% with the scheduling - the same place.}
\li{\strong{x86-64.} Every number here is aarch64. The arena's runner is x86-64: the size of the gain, the reproducibility and whether a profile recorded on aarch64 serves an x86-64 build have to be measured there before anything is promised (a profile is architecture-neutral by its format, section 3.3; that is not a test).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. What could not be established}

\ul{
\li{\strong{x86-64}: nothing. No such machine here; the Oracle GraalVM under \code{\verb~|.toolchain/~} is the aarch64 build.}
\li{\strong{A profile recorded on one architecture, used on the other}; \strong{a profile recorded with another Oracle GraalVM version} (the build has a version check and an option to ignore it; only 25.0.4 was at hand).}
\li{\strong{Wall clock and CPU time below about 5 \%.} Every timed cell is ONE run in a quiet window on a host whose other twelve cores were busy (load average 25 to 85); J1 found up to 5 \% between two identical 8-worker runs. The instruction counts are exact to the spreads shown.}
\li{\strong{How much of "Oracle without a profile against CE" is the H10 inline lists.} The CE image is built with the machine's lists, the Oracle images without (as \code{\verb~|build.sh~} builds them); an Oracle image with the lists was not built.}
\li{\strong{Cells that have a count and no clock, or nothing}: the windows were shared with eight to twelve other measurements in the queue, so only the CE image, the Oracle image without a profile and PGO A were timed on all eight corpora (PGO A with the reference engine on Mathlib only); PGO B and PGO C were timed on Mathlib and cedar and counted elsewhere; PGO B and PGO C were not run with the reference engine at all; the reference engine's own fresh and stale profiles (R, S1) were counted on Init, Std and cedar, not on Mathlib.}
\li{\strong{One worker}, and any worker count other than 8.}
\li{\strong{A stale profile at the distance of a release.} Two distances were measured, both inside this project's first two days, in which the code more than doubled within a day: 4.3 hours and 17 hours. A profile exactly one publication old was not measured: no jar was published between 00:02Z and the end of the series.}
\li{\strong{Whether the engine cache of Truffle returns compiled code in the Community Edition} (section 4): it stores and loads; the probe compiles its root node again after the load.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|--pgo-sampling~}} (profiles from \code{\verb~|perf~} samples instead of an instrumented image) and \strong{G1} (Oracle GraalVM only; [study-graalvm-platform](study-graalvm-platform) 7.4 found no instruction gain from it) were not tried.}
\li{\strong{The JVM launcher's instruction count on the arena's runner}: it depends on how the compiler threads are scheduled beside the workers (the counts of one cell differ by up to 10.9 \% here - on cedar; 4 \% on Mathlib - against 0.3 \% for an image with the machine), and on x86-64 it is unmeasured like everything else.}
\li{\strong{Whether the arena's maintainers accept a build line that downloads Oracle GraalVM}: not a measurement.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. Files and reproduction}

\pre{\verb~|
# images (5 + 9 minutes; one of the host's two native-image slots each)
bench/slot.sh -c ni -n 2 -- work/pg1/build.sh --published --pgo --opt 3 --dist work/pg1/dist --train ... (section 2)
work/pg1/scripts/oracle-image.sh ora                                  # Oracle, no profile (the control)
work/pg1/scripts/train.sh B init                                      # a further profile: work/pg1/profiles/B
work/pg1/scripts/oracle-image.sh pgoB --profiles work/pg1/profiles/B  # ... and its image: work/pg1/oimg/pgoB/rlvm
work/pg1/scripts/mkimg.sh pgoB work/pg1/oimg/pgoB/rlvm                # a checkout directory for the run line
# measurements
work/pg1/scripts/window.sh TAG pgoB cand mathlib                      # one quiet window: instructions, CPU, wall, RSS
work/pg1/scripts/warm.sh cand 4 mathlib pgoB                          # warm passes in a quiet window
work/pg1/scripts/count.sh TAG pgoB mdef cedar pgoB mdef init          # instruction counts, no window
work/pg1/scripts/suite.sh pgoA cand                                   # the 270 tests by the arena's procedure
python3 -I work/pg1/scripts/cmp-suites.py work/pg1/reports/suite.ce.cand.json work/pg1/reports/suite.pgoA.cand.json
python3 -I work/pg1/scripts/iprof-overlap.py work/pg1/profiles/Bd work/pg1/profiles/S2
# the JVM's counterpart of a training run, and the launcher with the cache under the run line's name
work/pg1/scripts/aot-train.sh init4 init 4 && work/pg1/scripts/window.sh TAG jvmaot-init4 cand mathlib
# this document: the tables, the quoted numbers, the text
python3 -I work/pg1/scripts/report.py                                 # every table
python3 -I work/pg1/scripts/tokens.py                                 # the numbers the prose quotes
python3 -I work/pg1/scripts/report.py --fill work/pg1/PG1.template.md docs/research/profiles/PG1.md
~}

\p{Files: \code{\verb~|work/pg1/reports/IMAGE.CONFIG.CORPUS.TAG.json~} (one measured run each: exit code, instructions, CPU, wall, RSS, whether it was inside a window, the run line), \code{\verb~|suite.IMAGE.CONFIG.json~} (270 tests each), \code{\verb~|work/pg1/warm.tsv~} and \code{\verb~|warm-logs/~}, \code{\verb~|work/pg1/profiles/*/~} (the \code{\verb~|.iprof~} files, the training logs, \code{\verb~|INFO.txt~}), \code{\verb~|work/pg1/oimg/*/~} (\code{\verb~|build.log~}, \code{\verb~|info.txt~}, the binaries), \code{\verb~|work/pg1/dist~} (the distribution of \code{\verb~|build.sh --pgo~}: CE image, PGO image A, JVM launcher, jars, \code{\verb~|BUILD-INFO.txt~}), \code{\verb~|work/pg1/logs/~} (window, chain and suite logs, \code{\verb~|repro-*.txt~}, \code{\verb~|m2-jars-before-build.txt~}), \code{\verb~|work/pg1/aot/~} (the JDK AOT caches and their training logs), \code{\verb~|work/pg1/stale-0756Z/~} (the profiles of the PGO build of 07:5xZ), \code{\verb~|work/pg1/scripts/~} (everything above), \code{\verb~|work/pg1/PG1.template.md~} and \code{\verb~|work/pg1/sec/~} (the text of this document).}
}
